messages:
Mina N: Heads-up: onboarding support tickets keep mentioning "missing fetch" or weird Response types when people follow the JS SDK quickstart. We need a canonical runtime fetch story (undici vs global fetch) that works for ESM/CJS, Next edge/server, Vite dev. Can we agree on a pattern so sample apps are less fragile?

Ethan (eng-sdk): My vote: prefer globalThis.fetch if available, otherwise dynamically import undici in Node. Key constraints: no top-level require('undici') so bundlers for edge builds don't pull it in. Minimal adapter: 
```js
export async function resolveFetch() {
  if (typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function') return globalThis.fetch;
  try {
    const mod = await import('undici');
    return mod.fetch;
  } catch (e) {
    throw new Error('No fetch: please run on Node >=18 or install undici/polyfill.');
  }
}
```
This keeps ESM/CJS consumers happy and avoids common bundler pitfalls.

Priya: Typing issues are real. When the SDK surface references Request/Response, TS will use lib.dom. But if users run in Node + undici, they sometimes get type mismatch warnings. Recommendation: publish our d.ts that only references standard web fetch types (lib.dom). If someone needs undici-specific types, they import them explicitly. Also mention cast examples in README.

Samir: For CJS compatibility add examples in README: 
```js
const { createClient } = require('@redwood/inference');
// works even if SDK internals use dynamic import for undici
```
And include a small helper snippet for older Node (16) showing how to polyfill: 
```js
// install: npm i undici
const { fetch } = require('undici');
globalThis.fetch = fetch;
```
This helps folks on legacy runtimes.

Tomas: CI additions: add matrix jobs to run tests under Node 16 (with undici), Node 18, Node 20, and a worker/edge simulated environment. Also add an integration sample that runs under Next server and Next edge to catch streaming and header differences.

Ethan (eng-sdk): Also: avoid re-exporting undici types in our package.json types entry. Keep undici as runtime optional dependency in docs only. In code, prefer the web Fetch API types. We'll add a small types file like fetch-shims.d.ts that does: 
```ts
// fetch-shims.d.ts
export type Fetch = (input: RequestInfo, init?: RequestInit) => Promise<Response>
```
and use that in internal signatures so consumers don't see undici in their autocomplete.

Priya: Bundler gotchas: Next sometimes provides a different implementation in dev vs production for edge functions; this can change streaming behavior. For Vite, add a note to exclude undici from optimizeDeps to avoid prebundling: 
```js
// vite.config.ts
export default defineConfig({
  optimizeDeps: { exclude: ['undici'] }
});
```
Also recommend sample app tests run in dev mode and prod build to catch these differences.

Mina N: Action items: 1) add runtime utils adapter in sdk-core (Ethan), 2) docs + sample app updates (I'll do), 3) CI matrix job (Tomas). Can we also add a tiny troubleshooting FAQ in README for TS mismatch and Vite/Next notes?

Ethan (eng-sdk): I'll open the adapter PR. Plan: runtime-utils/getFetch.ts, unit tests that stub globalThis.fetch and a fixture that installs undici. Also add a small ESM-friendly error message when dynamic import fails.

ci-bot: Job created: runtime-matrix/detect-fetch (requested by Ethan).

Samir: For release notes: "Improved fetch detection and undici fallback; better ESM/CJS guidance" + link to sample app. We'll also keep an onboarding checklist in the repo's README for common pitfalls.

Mina N: Thanks team. Let's keep the adapter minimal and surface clear error text for users. I'll draft the README FAQ and include the Vite/Next examples. :rocket:
